# Line endings are LF in the repository and in every checkout, on every OS.
#
# Without this, Git for Windows with its default `core.autocrlf=true` checked
# text files out with CRLF. Several things here read files line by line and
# anchor on the end of a line — `version_test.dart` finds the newest
# `## vX.Y.Z` heading in CHANGELOG.md with a `$`-anchored pattern, and a
# trailing `\r` made it match nothing — so the test suite was red on every
# Windows machine for reasons that had nothing to do with the app. The release
# workflow reads the same file on Linux and never saw it.
#
# Binary files are named so Git never tries to normalise them.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.webp binary
*.ico  binary
*.ttf  binary
*.otf  binary
*.jar  binary
*.zip  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.sqlite binary
*.nexbak binary

# Windows-native scripts keep the line endings their interpreters expect.
*.bat  text eol=crlf
*.cmd  text eol=crlf
*.ps1  text eol=crlf
